----------------------------------------------------------------------------
In ABAP, CORRESPONDING is used to copy matching fields between 
structures or internal tables based on same field names. 
It’s especially useful when you don’t want to manually assign each field.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Old Syntax (before modern ABAP)

*Earlier, you typically used MOVE-CORRESPONDING.
✔ Example

TYPES: BEGIN OF ty_source,
         name TYPE string,
         age  TYPE i,
       END OF ty_source.

TYPES: BEGIN OF ty_target,
         name TYPE string,
         age  TYPE i,
         city TYPE string,
       END OF ty_target.

DATA: ls_source TYPE ty_source,
      ls_target TYPE ty_target.

ls_source-name = 'John'.
ls_source-age  = 30.

MOVE-CORRESPONDING ls_source TO ls_target.


2. New Syntax (CORRESPONDING #( ))

*Modern ABAP (7.40+) introduced expression-based syntax.

ls_target = CORRESPONDING #( ls_source ).

✔ Same behavior as MOVE-CORRESPONDING, but cleaner and usable inside expressions.

3. Using MAPPING

Used when field names are different.

ls_target = CORRESPONDING #(
  ls_source
  MAPPING target_field = source_field
).
✔ Example
TYPES: BEGIN OF ty_source,
         fullname TYPE string,
         years    TYPE i,
       END OF ty_source.

TYPES: BEGIN OF ty_target,
         name TYPE string,
         age  TYPE i,
       END OF ty_target.

DATA(ls_source) = VALUE ty_source(
  fullname = 'ASNCODELAB'
  years    = 30
).

DATA(ls_target) = CORRESPONDING #(
  ls_source
  MAPPING
    name = fullname
    age  = years
).
Here:

fullname → name
years → age

4. Using EXCEPT

Used to exclude fields from being copied.
SYNTAX:
ls_target = CORRESPONDING #(
  ls_source
  EXCEPT field1 field2
).
✔ Example
TYPES: BEGIN OF ty_source,
         name TYPE string,
         age  TYPE i,
         salary TYPE i,
       END OF ty_source.

TYPES: BEGIN OF ty_target,
         name TYPE string,
         age  TYPE i,
         salary TYPE i,
       END OF ty_target.

DATA(ls_source) = VALUE ty_source(
  name = 'John'
  age = 30
  salary = 5000
).

DATA(ls_target) = CORRESPONDING #(
  ls_source
  EXCEPT salary
).

Result:

name, age copied
salary NOT copied


5. Using BASE

Used to keep existing values and update only matching ones.
SYNATX:
ls_target = CORRESPONDING #(
  BASE ( ls_target )
  ls_source
).
✔ Example
DATA(ls_target) = VALUE ty_target(
  name = 'ASN'
  age  = 20
  city = 'Bangalore'
).

DATA(ls_source) = VALUE ty_source(
  name = 'ASNCODELAB'
  age  = 30
).

ls_target = CORRESPONDING #(
  BASE ( ls_target )
  ls_source
).

Result:

name → updated to 'John'
age → updated to 30
city → remains 'Bangalore'
